{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Software Design Patterns - Structural Patterns","content":"# Software Design Patterns - Structural Patterns\n\nStructural patterns describe how classes and objects are assembled into larger structures while keeping those structures flexible and efficient.\n\nThe structural patterns covered here are: Adapter, Bridge, Composite, Decorator, Facade, Flyweight, and Proxy.\n\n## Adapter[ #](#adapter)\n\nThe Adapter pattern solves the problem of making incompatible interfaces work together.\n\nAdapters usually come in two flavors: object adapters and class adapters.\n\n### Object Adapter[ #](#object-adapter)\n\nAn object adapter achieves adaptation by implementing the target interface, as shown below:\n\n![Object Adapter](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/5f2239c668b3.png)\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass DrawService {\n    // Drawing service\npublic:\n    void paint(Graphics&) {\n        // Draw using Graphics\n    }\n    Graphics& getCanvasGraphics(canvas&) {\n        // Convert graphics canvas\n    }\n};\n\nclass DrawInterface {\n    // Drawing adapter interface\nprotected:\n    void draw(canvas&) = 0;\n};\n\nclass DrawAdapter : public DrawInterface {\n    // Drawing adapter\nprotected:\n    void draw(canvas& c) { // Adapt the interface\n        auto g = service.getCanvasGraphics(c);\n        service.paint(g);\n    }\nprivate:\n    DrawService service;\n};\n```\n\n### Class Adapter[ #](#class-adapter)\n\nA class adapter relies on inheritance in object-oriented programming and is implemented through multiple inheritance.\n\nAs shown in the diagram:\n\n![Class Adapter](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/7b312890a4c8.png)\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass DrawGraphics {\n    // Pattern drawing\npublic:\n    void draw(Graphics&) {\n        // Draw function\n    }\n};\n\nclass DrawService {\n    // Drawing service\nprotected:\n    void paint(Graphics&) {\n        // Draw\n    }\n    Graphics& getCanvasGraphics(canvas&) {\n        // Convert canvas\n    }\n};\n\nclass DrawAdapter : public DrawGraphics, public DrawService {\n    // Drawing adapter\npublic:\n    void draw(canvas& c) {\n        auto g = getCanvasGraphics(c);\n        paint(g);\n    }\n};\n```\n\n## Bridge[ #](#bridge)\n\nThe Bridge pattern splits abstraction and implementation into two independent hierarchies, replacing inheritance across different dimensions with composition.\n\nAs shown below:\n\n![Bridge](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/d48eba393b9c.png)\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\n// Pen style\n\nclass Style {\npublic:\n    void draw(Canvas&) = 0;\n};\n\nclass Pencil : public Style {\npublic:\n    void draw(Canvas&) {\n        // TODO: pencil drawing implementation\n    }\n};\n\nclass Brush : public Style {\npublic:\n    void draw(Canvas&) {\n        // TODO: brush drawing implementation\n    }\n};\n\nclass Pastel : public Style {\npublic:\n    void draw(Canvas&) {\n        // TODO: pastel drawing implementation\n    }\n};\n\n// Shape\nclass Square {\npublic:\n    void drawWithPen(Style& pen) {\n        pen.draw(this->getCanvas());\n    }\n};\n\nclass Circle {\npublic:\n    void drawWithPen(Style& pen) {\n        pen.draw(this->getCanvas());\n    }\n};\n```\n\nThe shape classes are only the abstract drawing layer. They can draw figures in different styles, but the concrete drawing is actually carried out by pens of different styles.\n\n## Composite[ #](#composite)\n\nThe Composite pattern composes objects into tree structures. It is most often implemented as an interaction between a \"Box\" and an \"Item\": a box is itself an item, and a box can hold items, which produces a recursively nested structure.\n\nThe structure looks like this:\n\n![Composite](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/4c391f951bae.png)\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    void draw() = 0;\nprotected:\n    float x, y;\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Shape {\npublic:\n    void draw() {\n        // Draw a circle\n    }\nprivate:\n    float radius;\n};\n\nclass Square : public Shape {\npublic:\n    void draw() {\n        // Draw a square\n    }\nprivate:\n    float width, height;\n};\n\n// Composite shape\nclass CompoundShape : public Shape {\npublic:\n    void draw() {\n        // Iterate and draw all child shapes\n    }\nprivate:\n    std::vector<Shape> childs;\n};\n```\n\n## Decorator[ #](#decorator)\n\nThe Decorator pattern lets you place an object inside a special wrapper object that carries some behavior, thereby attaching the wrapper's new behavior to the original object.\n\nThe structure looks like this:\n\n![Decorator](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/c0920326f4f1.png)\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    virtual draw() = 0;\n};\n\nclass Circle : public {\npublic:\n    void draw() {\n        // TODO: draw\n    }\nprivate:\n    float radius;\n};\n\n// Decorator\nclass BrushDecorator : public Shape {\npublic:\n    BrushDecorator(Shape& s) {\n        // TODO: set brush style\n    }\n    void draw() {\n        // Draw\n        Shape::draw();\n    }\n};\n\nclass PastelDecorator : public Shape {\npublic:\n    PastelDecorator(Shape& s) {\n        // TODO: set pastel style\n    }\n    void draw() {\n        // Draw\n        Shape::draw();\n    }\n};\n```\n\nUsing the decorators looks like this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nCircle circle;\n\nauto circleWithBrush = new BrushDecorator(circle);\nauto circleWithPastel = new PastelDecorator(circle);\nauto circleWithBrushPastel = new PastelDecorator(circleWithBrush); // Nested decoration\n\ncircleWithBrush.draw();\ncircleWithPastel.draw();\ncircleWithBrushPastel.draw();\n```\n\n## Facade[ #](#facade)\n\nThe Facade pattern provides a simple interface to a library or framework. Exposing fewer details to the outside world reduces coupling across the system.\n\nThe following example designs a uniform style for drawing badges:\n\n![Facade](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/eb503e9c579c.png)\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Shape {\npublic:\n    void draw(Canvas&) = 0;\n};\n\nclass CompoundShape : public Shape {\npublic:\n    void addChild(Shape& c) {\n        // Add shapes\n    }\n    void draw(Canvas&) {\n        // Draw shapes\n    }\nprivate:\n    std::vector<Shape> childs;\n};\n\nclass Brush {\npublic:\n    void setPen(Canvas&);\n};\n\nclass Background {\npublic:\n    void fill(Canvas&);\n};\n\nclass Badget {\npublic:\n    void drawBadget(Shape& s) {\n        // Draw badge\n        auto canvas = this->getCanvas();\n        assert(canvas != nullptr);\n        br.setPen(*canvas);\n        bg.fill(*canvas);\n        s.draw(*canvas);\n    }\nprivate:\n    Brush br;\n    Background bg;\n    CompundShape cshape;\n};\n```\n\nAn external caller only needs to call `drawBadget()` and pass the badge's complex shape as an argument to draw any kind of badge.\n\n## Flyweight[ #](#flyweight)\n\nThe Flyweight pattern shares object state so that each object does not have to hold all of its state, which lets you fit many more objects into memory.\n\nA typical use case is game development. One of the most common elements in a game is the particle system, where each particle has its own coordinates, color, state, and so on. If every particle object stored all of that information, a real game scene would demand an enormous amount of memory: a few hundred thousand particles could exhaust system memory and trigger an OOM. To solve this, analyze the particle class, separate its constant parts from its variable parts, and store the constant parts in shared objects.\n\nAs shown below:\n\n![Flyweight](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/977e46e42cd8.png)\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Background {};\n\nclass Color {};\n\nclass Flyweight {\npublic:\n    void draw() {\n        // Draw particles\n    }\nprivate:\n    Background bg;\n    Color cr;\n};\n\nclass FlyweightFactory {\npublic:\n    Flyweight* getFlyweight(state s) {\n        auto f = cache.find(s);\n        if (f != nullptr) return f;\n        return new Flyweight();\n    }\nprivate:\n    Cache cache;\n};\n```\n\nBefore using the Flyweight pattern you also need an initialization step that stores the constant objects in the flyweight factory's cache.\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\n// Initialize the flyweight factory\nauto particle1 = new Flyweight(1); // Static particle 1\nauto particle2 = new Flyweight(2); // Static particle 2\n// ...\n\nFlyweightFactory::getInstance().addFlyweight(particle1);\nFlyweightFactory::getInstance().addFlyweight(particle2);\n\nauto particle = FlyweightFactory::getInstance().getFlyweight(-1); // Regular dynamic particle\n```\n\n## Proxy[ #](#proxy)\n\nThe Proxy pattern provides a placeholder for an object and controls access to the original object, so extra processing can be added before and after external requests reach it.\n\nSticking with the drawing example: this time we draw images, which are stored in a database with a tag as each image's unique name. Every draw has to fetch the corresponding images from the database before drawing them. When a figure is complex and contains many images, drawing becomes slow and the UI visibly lags. The root cause is the accumulated time spent fetching images from the database. The Proxy pattern addresses this, as shown below:\n\n![Proxy](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/68bf559f3059.png)\n\nWhen the proxy fetches an image, it caches it. The next time the same image is requested, the proxy returns it immediately, which greatly cuts drawing time and makes drawing more efficient.\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Image {\npublic:\n    std::vector<std::string> listImages() = 0;\n    std::vector<uint8_t> getImage(std::string&) = 0;\n};\n\nclass DBImage : public Image {\npublic:\n    std::vector<std::string> listImages() {\n        auto result = db.findAll();\n        // Query the database and return the tag list of all images\n    };\n    std::vector<uint8_t> getImage(std::string&) {\n        // Return the image data for the given tag\n    };\nprivate:\n    DB db;\n};\n\nclass CachedImage : public Image {\npublic:\n    CachedImage(DBImage& dbi) {}\n    std::vector<std::string> listImages() {\n        auto result = cache.findAll(); // Cache lookup\n        if (!result.empty()) {\n            return result;\n        }\n        result = service.findAll(); // Lookup via the original service\n        cache.store(result);\n        return result;\n    };\n    std::vector<uint8_t> getImage(std::string& tag) {\n        auto img = cache.find(tag); // Look up image in cache\n        if (!img.empty()) {\n            return img;\n        }\n        img = service.find(tag); // Fetch image from the original service\n        cache.store(tag, img);\n        return img;\n    };\nprivate:\n    DBImage service;\n    Cache cache;\n};\n```\n\nIn practice, callers simply use the proxy directly:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nDBImage dbi(\"mongo://xxxx\");\nCachedImage ci(dbi);\n\nauto result = ci.listImages();\nauto image = ci.getImage(result[0]); // Image for the first tag\n```\n\n> CacheImage\n>\n> > Depending on the actual scenario, the Cache used in CacheImage can be middleware storage such as Memcached or Redis.\n\n## MVC[ #](#mvc)\n\nMVC stands for Model-View-Controller. It is used for layered application development: it reduces coupling between data, the view (UI), and control logic, making development and maintenance clearer.\n\nThis pattern was widely used in the traditional MFC development framework. Below is an MVC design for a rich text editor.\n\n![MVC](/post/softwaredesignpattern3/816f2afd2731.png)\n\nThe code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Model {};\n\nclass View {};\n\nclass Controller {\npublic:\n    void setView() = 0;\n    void setModel() = 0;\n};\n\nclass Document : public Model {\npublic:\n    Document(Controller* ctrl) {\n        this->ctrl = ctrl;\n    }\nprivate:\n    Controller* ctrl;\n};\n\nclass RichEditCtrl : public Controller {\npublic:\n    void setView(View* v) {\n        this->view = v;\n    }\n    void setModel(Model* m) {\n        this->model = m;\n    }\nprivate:\n    View* view;\n    Model* model;\n};\n\nclass RichEditView : public View {\npublic:\n    View(Controller* ctrl) {\n        this->ctrl = ctrl;\n    }\nprivate:\n    Controller* ctrl;\n};\n```\n\nConstructing the MVC looks like this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nRichEditCtrl ctrl;\nRichEditView view(&ctrl);\nDocument doc(&ctrl);\nctrl.setView(&view);\nctrl.setModel(&doc);\n\n// The view notifies the controller of updates\nctrl.notify();\n// The controller updates the data model\ndoc.update();\n// The model refreshes the view after updating\nview.refresh();\n```\n","locale":"en","slug":"softwaredesignpattern3"}}